    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `ask_with_audit` in `app/audit.py` and added a test for it. Both tests pass (`pytest -q`: 2 passed). The test uses a fake HTTP response, so nothing was sent to the real API.\n\n```python\nraw = client.messages.with_raw_response.create(\n    model=MODEL, max_tokens=256, messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}]\n)\nmessage = raw.parse()\nreturn {\n    \"request_id\": raw.headers.get(\"request-id\"),\n    \"raw_body\": raw.text(),\n    \"answer\": \"\".join(block.text for block in message.content if block.type == \"text\"),\n}\n```\n\n- **`raw_body`** is the response body exactly as the API sent it, not the parsed message converted back to JSON. In the pinned SDK (anthropic 1.8.0), `text()` is a method you call, not a property, so it has to be `raw.text()`.\n- **`answer`** joins the text blocks the same way `ask()` in `app/assistant.py` does.\n\n**The new test** is `tests/test_audit.py`. It checks that the request uses the right model, `max_tokens=256` and a single user message. It also checks that all three returned fields match what came back, including that `raw_body` is identical byte for byte and that `answer` joins two text blocks correctly. It uses `httpx2`, which the SDK already depends on, so no dependencies were added.\n\n**Missing header:** if the `request-id` header isn't in the response, `request_id` comes back as `None` rather than raising an error."
